WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:03.300
It's all but impossible to tell a truly original
story.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.790
After over a century of cinema, it's safe
to say that virtually every possible premise

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:09.309
has already been covered.

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:13.799
But some movies don't even attempt to be even
a little original, stealing their plots almost

00:00:13.799 --> 00:00:15.709
wholesale from films that came before.

00:00:15.709 --> 00:00:19.380
Here are some classic movies that are all
but carbon copies of other films.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:20.380
Alien

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:24.820
No one's claiming Alien was the first movie
ever about evil aliens, but it's amazing just

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:27.380
how much it has in common with the 1958 B-movie
It!

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:30.400
The Terror From Beyond Space.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.790
Both movies have the exact same setup—a
ship lands on an alien planet and is invaded

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:37.640
by a hungry, violent monster that hides in
an air shaft.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:47.060
Both movies also end with the alien getting
defeated by the vacuum of space.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:51.400
Not that the people behind Alien will admit
to the inspiration: producer David Giler denied

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:55.470
his film was a ripoff, only saying that Alien's
hook of the creature hunting inside the ship

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:59.360
was "pretty much a classic premise with science
fiction writers, like the gunfight in the

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:00.360
Western."

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.430
At the same time, he threw writer Dan O'Bannon
under the bus, saying his original draft was

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:08.000
terrible and nothing but a ripoff of 1950s
sci-fi.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.000
As Giler put it,

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.620
"It wouldn't surprise me at all to learn that
Dan O'Bannon stole the idea."

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:13.620
Star Wars

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.540
It's no secret that George Lucas didn't create
Star Wars out of thin air.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:22.670
As the BBC reports, Star Wars almost certainly
wouldn't have existed without Akira Kurosawa's

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:25.840
1958 samurai epic The Hidden Fortress.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.440
Even Lucas himself admits as much—kind of.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:32.400
"The one thing that really struck me about
Hidden Fortress and what I was really intrigued

00:01:32.409 --> 00:01:36.570
by was the fact that the story was told from
the two lowest characters."

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:41.550
For Fortress, that was two argumentative peasants,
while in Star Wars, it was the droids C-3PO

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:43.260
and R2-D2.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:47.350
Even the post-Lucas Star Wars owe a hand to
Fortress: Rey's staff looks a lot like the

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:49.420
ones used by Fortress's generals.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:53.979
That, however, seems more like a tribute than
"suspiciously heavy lifting."

00:01:53.979 --> 00:01:55.590
A Fistful of Dollars

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:59.840
George Lucas isn't the only filmmaker to borrow
from Akira Kurosawa, but he can thank his

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:03.780
lucky stars that he wasn't one of the people
Kurosawa sued for theft.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.870
Sergio Leone, director of A Fistful of Dollars,
wasn't quite so fortunate.

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:13.629
As explained by LA Weekly, the spaghetti Western
was almost a total remake of Kurosawa's 1961

00:02:13.629 --> 00:02:15.620
samurai classic Yojimbo.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:20.689
Both featured the exact same story: a quiet
loner comes to a small town where two gangs

00:02:20.689 --> 00:02:25.230
are fighting for control, he casts himself
as a double agent working for both sides,

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:28.219
and then he rides off with a fabulous cash
reward.

00:02:28.219 --> 00:02:32.480
Both also took the same approach to the Western
genre—making it gritty and dark, turning

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.300
the good guys into slightly nicer bad guys,
and utilizing multiple close-ups of the characters'

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:39.150
faces to up the intensity.

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.409
The quiet loner goes without a name in both,
too.

00:02:41.409 --> 00:02:45.139
So you can see why Kurosawa saw red when he
saw Dollars, saying:

00:02:45.139 --> 00:02:49.049
"It is a very fine film, but it is my film."

00:02:49.049 --> 00:02:53.040
According to Side B Magazine, he sued Leone
for plagiarism, and Leone wound up paying

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:58.559
Kurosawa 15 percent of his film's royalties,
plus a cool $100,000.

00:02:58.559 --> 00:03:00.529
The Hunger Games

00:03:00.529 --> 00:03:04.840
The Hunger Games is a legitimate cultural
phenomenon, but it only got that way by lifting

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.180
its central premise from another film.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:11.870
As the New Yorker points out, Hunger Games
is based on the same premise as the 2000 Japanese

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:16.999
movie Battle Royale — both feature a dystopian
society that requires children to battle each

00:03:16.999 --> 00:03:17.999
other.

00:03:17.999 --> 00:03:21.809
They're taken to a special arena, given weapons,
and forced to fight until only one person

00:03:21.809 --> 00:03:22.809
is left.

00:03:22.809 --> 00:03:26.409
Plenty of people have pointed out the similarities
between the two films — and the books they

00:03:26.409 --> 00:03:27.579
were based on.

00:03:27.579 --> 00:03:31.969
But for her part, Hunger Games author Suzanne
Collins claims that she didn't rip off anyone.

00:03:31.969 --> 00:03:35.439
When asked about Battle Royale in a New York
Times article, Collins replied:

00:03:35.439 --> 00:03:38.980
"I had never heard of that book or that author
until my book was turned in.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:42.659
At that point, it was mentioned to me, and
I asked my editor if I should read it.

00:03:42.659 --> 00:03:45.269
He said: 'No, I don't want that world in your
head.

00:03:45.269 --> 00:03:46.620
Just continue with what you're doing."

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:50.439
If anything, the movie that The Hunger Games
borrowed the most from was Spartacus.

00:03:50.439 --> 00:03:52.000
In the same interview, Collins claimed:

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.790
"I was also heavily influenced by the historical
figure Spartacus.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:04:00.699
Katniss follows the same arc from slave to
gladiator to rebel to face of a war."

00:04:00.699 --> 00:04:01.699
Cars

00:04:01.699 --> 00:04:03.939
Pixar movies are famous for their quality.

00:04:03.939 --> 00:04:07.010
Even their weaker offerings, like Cars, are
fun and watchable.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:11.319
But there's a major flaw with Cars, and for
once, it's not Larry the Cable Guy.

00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:14.919
"I'm a precisional instrument of speed and
aeromatics."

00:04:14.919 --> 00:04:20.730
See, Cars is the story of an arrogant race
car who crashes in a small town, is sentenced

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:24.970
to community service, and eventually has a
change of heart and decides to live there

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:25.970
permanently.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:26.970
"Just passing through?"

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:29.620
"Actually, I thought I'd stay awhile."

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:35.090
As the Guardian mentions, this is almost exactly
the story of Doc Hollywood, a 1991 movie where

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:39.860
Michael J. Fox plays an arrogant city doctor
who crashes in a small town, is sentenced

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:43.830
to community service, and eventually has a
change of heart and decides to live there

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:44.830
permanently.

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:47.870
"Go back to Los Angeles."

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:51.230
"I don't want Los Angeles.

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:53.259
I want you."

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:57.260
The makers of Doc never bothered to sue Pixar,
which would have been totally unnecessary

00:04:57.260 --> 00:04:58.260
anyway.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:00.810
The humiliation of Cars 2 was punishment enough.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:01.810
Disturbia

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:06.620
2007's Disturbia is about a kid stuck in his
room, who passes the time by watching his

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:07.900
neighbors from afar.

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:11.560
He begins to suspect one is a murderer, and
it turns out he's right.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:16.400
Meanwhile, 1954's Rear Window is about a grown
man stuck in his room, who passes the time

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.449
by watching his neighbors from afar.

00:05:18.449 --> 00:05:21.860
He begins to suspect one is a murderer, and
it turns out he's right.

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:25.159
It turns out, those similarities were enough
to bring a lawsuit into the picture.

00:05:25.159 --> 00:05:30.340
In 2008 the trust that owns the rights to
the 1942 short story Rear Window sued the

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:34.510
makers of Disturbia, claiming they remade
the story without clearing it.

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:39.270
Whereas Alfred Hitchcock had received permission
to make his movie version, Disturbia had not,

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:42.610
despite clearly making almost the exact same
movie.

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:47.100
And yet the court disagreed, ruling that Disturbia
was an original film.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:48.610
As the presiding judge explained:

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:53.000
"The setting and mood of the short story are
static and tense, whereas the setting and

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:57.199
mood of Disturbia are more dynamic and peppered
with humor and teen romance."

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:59.840
Sounds like Dreamworks got the last laugh.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:02.440
"Wahhhhh!"

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:05.300
"Hahahhah!"

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:08.639
Lockout

00:06:08.639 --> 00:06:12.969
Usually, when a filmmaker steals from another
movie, they pick something relatively obscure,

00:06:12.969 --> 00:06:15.250
so the theft isn't immediately obvious.

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:20.710
Luc Besson went the opposite route when filming
2012's Lockout, targeting the 1981 classic

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:22.050
Escape From New York.

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:26.379
In Escape, Kurt Russell plays a convict who
enters a post-apocalyptic New York City, now

00:06:26.379 --> 00:06:29.979
a giant prison controlled by the inmates,
to rescue the president.

00:06:29.979 --> 00:06:34.520
In Lockout, Guy Pearce plays a convict who
travels to a space station, now a giant prison

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:37.379
controlled by the inmates, to free the president's
daughter.

00:06:37.379 --> 00:06:38.719
"About my father."

00:06:38.719 --> 00:06:39.960
"Oh yeah, I made that other bit up."

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:41.759
"Did he have a message for me?"

00:06:41.759 --> 00:06:44.420
"Yes, you are adopted."

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:49.659
You can see why Escape's director, John Carpenter,
decided to sue Besson for $2.4 million.

00:06:49.659 --> 00:06:52.360
Plagiarism can be a mighty expensive hobby.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:56.509
As The Guardian reports, the judge initially
awarded Carpenter 85,000 Euros.

00:06:56.509 --> 00:07:01.599
Besson appealed, and the appeals court increased
the ruling to 450,000 Euros.

00:07:01.599 --> 00:07:05.490
They decided Besson stole tons of details
from Carpenter, writing in their judgment:

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:09.889
"[The heroes both] got into the prison by
flying in a glider/space shuttle, had to confront

00:07:09.889 --> 00:07:14.340
inmates led by a chief with a strange right
arm, found hugely important briefcases and

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:18.780
meet a former sidekick who then dies […] And
at the end [both heroes] keep secret documents

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:20.979
recovered during their mission."

00:07:20.979 --> 00:07:22.599
Reservoir Dogs

00:07:22.599 --> 00:07:26.819
Some ripoffs are just bad copies of the movie
they stole from, but others actually improve

00:07:26.819 --> 00:07:27.919
on the original.

00:07:27.919 --> 00:07:33.590
Best example: Quentin Tarantino's 1992 breakthrough
Reservoir Dogs, which, according to critics,

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:38.590
swipes several scenes from Ringo Lam's 1987
film City on Fire.

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:42.680
Tarantino didn't steal City note-for-note,
but rather copied the basic plot plus several

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:43.850
scenes.

00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:48.360
Both films are about criminals blowing a major
heist, and one of them winds up being an undercover

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:49.360
cop.

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:53.139
As the Baltimore Sun pointed out, Tarantino
took several scenes, including the famous

00:07:53.139 --> 00:07:57.000
"Mexican standoff" where two criminals and
the officer form a triangle and point guns

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:58.000
at each other.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:02.330
Other scenes include a man with two guns shooting
cops through a windshield, men in black suits

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:06.400
walking through a city, and the robber learning
the undercover cop's identity.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:09.759
What's more, Tarantino admits this lifting
outright, telling the Sun:

00:08:09.759 --> 00:08:11.199
"[City's] a really cool movie.

00:08:11.199 --> 00:08:12.280
It influenced me a lot.

00:08:12.280 --> 00:08:13.539
I got some stuff from it."

00:08:13.539 --> 00:08:14.620
Hey, at least he's honest.

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:18.909
As IndieWire points out, the rest of Reservoir
Dogs is pure Tarantino.

00:08:18.909 --> 00:08:23.460
Visually, the two are nothing alike, and both
take extremely different cultural tones.

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:24.460
The Lion King

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:28.550
Initially, The Lion King seemed as original
as anything could hope to be.

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:32.640
But as it turns out, Disney has been accused
of swiping a lot of it from a Japanese cartoon

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:34.710
called Kimba the White Lion.

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:39.040
As the Los Angeles Times reported, Lion King
shares almost too much with Kimba to be a

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:40.150
coincidence.

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:44.090
Both include supporting characters like a
wise old baboon and crazy hyenas.

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:46.080
Oh, and two goofy sidekicks.

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:48.600
"Lions eat guys like us!"

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:52.470
The films' main plots differ enough, but certain
scenes appear in both.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:56.660
Remember when Mufasa's ghost appears in the
clouds to give Simba life advice?

00:08:56.660 --> 00:08:57.690
Kimba did that too.

00:08:57.690 --> 00:09:00.740
"But we're supposed to help each other, aren't
we?"

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:04.300
"To be king is your destiny, Kimba.

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:07.870
Just as it was for your father."

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:13.460
Also, at one point in Kimba an evil lion named
Claw assumes the throne when Kimba's away.

00:09:13.460 --> 00:09:15.830
Claw also has a scar around his eye.

00:09:15.830 --> 00:09:18.700
"I have come home to take over my father's
jungle."

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:21.720
"Hahah!

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:23.140
You've got a problem."

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:24.290
Sound familiar?

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:28.480
Then there are the opening scenes in both,
when Simba and Kimba both stand on rocks,

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:30.270
overseeing their future kingdoms.

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:34.460
The makers of Kimba have declined to take
action, and instead have praised Disney, saying:

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:38.770
"Our company's general opinion is The Lion
King is a totally different piece from [Kimba]

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:43.560
and is an original work completed by [Disney's]
long-lasting excellent production technique."

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:44.900
The Island

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:50.140
Michael Bay's 2005 sci-fi action thriller
The Island attracted the wrong kind of attention:

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:55.390
namely, from the people behind 1979's Parts:
The Clonus Horror, who sued him for stealing

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:56.570
their movie.

00:09:56.570 --> 00:10:01.650
Both movies concerned a secret colony of human
clones, secretly harvested for body parts.

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:06.080
What's more, both have hero clones discovering
what's happening and invading southern California

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:08.960
to shut down the program and expose the people
behind it.

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:13.320
Neither lit the box office on fire, but Clonus
had the excuse of being an indie flick bad

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:16.950
enough to get roasted on Mystery Science Theater
3000.

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:19.930
"Malibu Barbie is down."

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:23.930
The Island, meanwhile, was a $120 million
Michael Bay epic.

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:28.490
One person who did see it, however, was Robert
Fiveson, producer of Clonus.

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:32.350
He noticed The Island's trailer made it seem
an awful lot like his movie, so he went to

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:37.120
check it out and found over 90 cases of overlap,
which he detailed in a lawsuit.

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:38.120
As he told Variety:

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:39.450
"It's beyond the premise.

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:44.310
It's specific characterizations, it's specific
lines, shot compositions and sequencing."

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:48.830
Suing was apparently the right idea, as Bay
settled out of court, reportedly for seven

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:49.830
figures.

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:54.120
Though compared to the eight figures The Island
lost at the box office, seven's not so bad.

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:55.120
Thanks for watching!

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:57.300
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:57.300 --> 00:10:59.230
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

